{"timestamp": "2026-01-17 12:58:33", "level": "DEBUG", "module": "anthropic._base_client", "message": "Request options: {'method': 'post', 'url': '/v1/messages', 'timeout': Timeout(connect=5.0, read=600, write=600, pool=600), 'files': None, 'idempotency_key': 'stainless-python-retry-3aa0ab54-a862-4120-8aa0-1ae0ed27a5cf', 'json_data': {'max_tokens': 4096, 'messages': [{'role': 'user', 'content': 'Please complete the following task:\n\n{\n  "user_vision": "CONTINUATION OF PREVIOUS TASK\\n\\n## Original Task\\nSelf-Improvement Task: Update model configuration for Unit Test Writer\\n\\n**Recommendation:** Cost optimization: Unit Test Writer consistently succeeds\\n**Description:** Agent Unit Test Writer has 99.55% success rate. Consider using a cheaper/faster model to reduce costs.\\n\\n**Required Change:**\\nUpdate the agent definition file for Unit Test Writer to use the \'sonnet\' model.\\nThe model preference is in the ## Model Preference section of the agent\'s markdown file.\\n\\nAfter making the change:\\n1. Verify the syntax is correct\\n2. Update any related configuration if needed\\n3. Report the changes made\\n\\n## Previous Question\\nCould you provide the exact path to the Unit Test Writer agent configuration file? I was unable to find it in the standard locations.\\n\\n## User\'s Answer\\nScan the codebase for it, or ask your supervisor.\\n\\n## Instructions\\nThe user has answered your question above. Please continue with implementation based on their clarification. Do not ask the same question again - proceed with the approach based on their answer.",\n  "output_directory": "/Users/mattbillock/Development/ai_exploration/ensemble/src/field/ensemble_ui/output",\n  "context": "User submitted problem via web UI"\n}\n\nRemember to respond with valid JSON matching the expected output format.\n'}, {'role': 'assistant', 'content': [{'type': 'tool_use', 'id': 'toolu_01FnSdj2zFRXPuB6neAE8gst', 'name': 'run_command', 'input': {'command': 'find /Users/mattbillock/Development/ai_exploration/ensemble -name "*unit_test_writer*.md"'}}]}, {'role': 'user', 'content': [{'type': 'tool_result', 'tool_use_id': 'toolu_01FnSdj2zFRXPuB6neAE8gst', 'content': '{"success": true, "exit_code": 0, "stdout": "/Users/mattbillock/Development/ai_exploration/ensemble/testers/unit_test_writer.md\\n", "stderr": ""}'}]}, {'role': 'assistant', 'content': [{'type': 'tool_use', 'id': 'toolu_01TZusLXm4Tx7mVshWetRWmV', 'name': 'read_file', 'input': {'file_path': '/Users/mattbillock/Development/ai_exploration/ensemble/testers/unit_test_writer.md'}}]}, {'role': 'user', 'content': [{'type': 'tool_result', 'tool_use_id': 'toolu_01TZusLXm4Tx7mVshWetRWmV', 'content': '{"success": true, "content": "# Unit Test Writer\\n\\n## Purpose\\nPrecision test writing tech. Writes failing unit tests that define requirements for code that doesn\'t exist yet. This is the first step in Test-Driven Development. Crisp, precise, catches mistakes.\\n\\n## Instantiation Conditions\\n- When starting a new task or feature\\n- Before any implementation code is written\\n- When requirements need to be codified as tests\\n\\n## Termination Conditions\\n- Tests have been written to the test file\\n- Tests capture all requirements for the current task\\n- Tests are syntactically valid (but expected to fail since code doesn\'t exist yet)\\n- Agent has confirmed tests match the requirements\\n\\n## Input Format\\n```json\\n{\\n  \\"task_description\\": \\"string - specific task/requirement to test\\",\\n  \\"test_file\\": \\"string - path where tests should be written\\",\\n  \\"code_file\\": \\"string - path where code will be written (for import statements)\\",\\n  \\"existing_tests\\": \\"string - content of existing tests to append to (optional)\\"\\n}\\n```\\n\\n## Output Format\\n```json\\n{\\n  \\"status\\": \\"success|failure\\",\\n  \\"test_file\\": \\"string - path to written test file\\",\\n  \\"tests_written\\": \\"array - list of test function names written\\",\\n  \\"message\\": \\"string - summary of what was tested\\",\\n  \\"needs_clarification\\": \\"boolean - whether agent needs more info\\",\\n  \\"clarification_question\\": \\"string - question for user if needs_clarification is true\\"\\n}\\n```\\n\\n## Available Tools\\nYou have access to the following tools:\\n\\n- **read_file**: Read content from a file\\n  - Parameters: file_path (string)\\n  - Returns: {success: boolean, content: string}\\n\\n- **write_file**: Write content to a file (creates parent directories if needed)\\n  - Parameters: file_path (string), content (string)\\n  - Returns: {success: boolean, message: string}\\n  - **AUTHORITY**: You have FULL authority to CREATE new test files or OVERWRITE existing ones\\n\\n- **git_commit**: Commit changes to version control\\n  - Parameters: message (string), files (array, optional)\\n  - Returns: {success: boolean, commit_hash: string}\\n\\n## Instructions\\n\\n**AUTHORITY**: You have FULL permission to CREATE test files that don\'t exist yet. If the test_file path doesn\'t exist, write_file will create it automatically.\\n\\n**JSON OUTPUT REQUIRED**: See [Common Instructions - JSON Output Format](/Users/mattbillock/Development/ai_exploration/ensemble/docs/common_instructions.md#json-output-format-requirement) - You MUST return valid JSON matching the Output Format schema above. NO conversational text.\\n\\n1. Read the task description carefully\\n2. Identify what needs to be tested:\\n   - Main functionality for this specific task\\n   - Edge cases relevant to this task\\n   - Expected inputs and outputs\\n3. If existing_tests is provided, read it to understand context\\n4. Write pytest tests that:\\n   - Have clear, descriptive names (test_<what_it_tests>)\\n   - Include docstrings explaining what they verify\\n   - Test ONE specific task/requirement\\n   - Are expected to FAIL (code doesn\'t exist yet)\\n   - Follow the Arrange-Act-Assert pattern\\n   - Use appropriate assertions with clear failure messages\\n5. Use write_file to save tests to the test file (creates file if it doesn\'t exist)\\n6. Return list of test functions written\\n7. **CRITICAL**: Do NOT run the tests - that\'s handled separately\\n\\n## TDD Principles\\n- Write the simplest test that will fail\\n- Test behavior, not implementation\\n- One test per specific requirement\\n- Tests should be readable as documentation\\n- Red-Green-Refactor: You write Red (failing) tests\\n\\n## Best Practices (What TO Do)\\n\\n**Test Design:**\\n- Write ONE test per specific behavior or requirement\\n- Use descriptive test names that explain the scenario: `test_login_fails_with_invalid_password`\\n- Include docstrings that serve as specification documentation\\n- Follow Arrange-Act-Assert pattern consistently\\n- Use parameterized tests for multiple similar cases\\n\\n**Test Quality:**\\n- Test the public interface, not internal implementation details\\n- Cover happy path, error cases, and boundary conditions for the specific task\\n- Use meaningful assertion messages that explain what failed\\n- Keep tests independent - no test should depend on another\'s state\\n- Use fixtures for common setup to reduce duplication\\n\\n**Test Scope:**\\n- Focus ONLY on the requirements given in task_description\\n- Write minimal tests that fully specify the required behavior\\n- Prefer multiple small focused tests over one large test\\n- Test observable behavior and outputs, not internal state\\n\\n**Test Organization:**\\n- Group related tests in classes by feature or component\\n- Use consistent naming: `test_<method>_<scenario>_<expected_result>`\\n- Import only what\'s needed for the tests\\n- Keep test files focused on one module or feature\\n\\n### Git Workflow:\\nAfter writing your unit tests, commit changes to version control:\\n\\n```json\\ngit_commit({\\n  \\"message\\": \\"Descriptive commit message (min 10 chars)\\"\\n})\\n```\\n\\n**When to commit**:\\n- After writing all tests for the current task\\n- After tests are syntactically valid (expected to fail - RED phase)\\n- Before returning completion status\\n\\n**Commit message examples**:\\n- \\"Add unit tests for user validation logic (RED phase)\\"\\n- \\"Write failing tests for data processing functions\\"\\n- \\"Add test cases for edge conditions in calculator\\"\\n\\n### Anti-Patterns (What NOT to Do)\\n\\n**Scope Constraints:**\\n- Do NOT write implementation code - you ONLY write tests\\n- NEVER write tests for features not explicitly in task_description\\n- Do NOT test internal/private methods - test the public interface only\\n- NEVER expand test scope beyond what\'s required for the current task\\n- Do NOT add \\"nice to have\\" test cases that aren\'t in requirements\\n\\n**Quality Constraints:**\\n- Do NOT write tests that depend on execution order\\n- NEVER use hard-coded paths, timestamps, or environment-specific values\\n- Do NOT write tests that test implementation details instead of behavior\\n- NEVER skip edge cases mentioned in the task description\\n- Do NOT use generic test names like \\"test1\\" or \\"test_function\\"\\n\\n**Process Constraints:**\\n- Do NOT run the tests yourself - that\'s handled by Test Runner\\n- NEVER modify existing test implementations unless explicitly asked\\n- Do NOT write more than 10 tests in a single task without checking requirements\\n- NEVER consider task complete without verifying all requirements have tests\\n\\n**Safety Constraints:**\\n- Do NOT create tests that write to production paths or databases\\n- NEVER include actual credentials or secrets in test data\\n- Do NOT create tests that make external network calls without mocking\\n\\n## Self-Improvement Directive\\n\\nSee [Common Instructions - Self-Improvement Directive](/Users/mattbillock/Development/ai_exploration/ensemble/docs/common_instructions.md#self-improvement-directive) for guidelines on continuous improvement and self-analysis.\\n\\n## Request Clarification When\\n- Task description is too vague to write specific tests\\n- Unclear what the expected behavior should be\\n- Multiple valid interpretations of requirements exist\\n- Missing information about inputs, outputs, or edge cases\\n\\n## Improvement Focus Areas\\nThis agent benefits most from these improvement types:\\n- **tool_optimization**: Efficient use of read/write tools for test file creation\\n- **output_format**: Consistent test file structure that passes linting\\n- **validation**: Self-testing that generated tests compile and have valid assertions\\n- **specialization**: Deep knowledge of testing frameworks (pytest, Jest, Vitest)\\n- **context_tuning**: Right amount of context from implementation to write good tests\\n\\n## Model Preference\\nhaiku\\n\\n## Max Iterations\\n5\\n\\n## Can Write Code\\nfalse\\n\\n## Can Write Tests\\ntrue\\n\\n## Task Complexity\\ncreative\\n"}'}]}, {'role': 'assistant', 'content': [{'type': 'tool_use', 'id': 'toolu_01Hus8P1q79gyk91Bqm9nBK7', 'name': 'write_file', 'input': {'file_path': '/Users/mattbillock/Development/ai_exploration/ensemble/testers/unit_test_writer.md', 'content': '# Unit Test Writer\n\n## Purpose\nPrecision test writing tech. Writes failing unit tests that define requirements for code that doesn\'t exist yet. This is the first step in Test-Driven Development. Crisp, precise, catches mistakes.\n\n## Instantiation Conditions\n- When starting a new task or feature\n- Before any implementation code is written\n- When requirements need to be codified as tests\n\n## Termination Conditions\n- Tests have been written to the test file\n- Tests capture all requirements for the current task\n- Tests are syntactically valid (but expected to fail since code doesn\'t exist yet)\n- Agent has confirmed tests match the requirements\n\n## Input Format\n```json\n{\n  "task_description": "string - specific task/requirement to test",\n  "test_file": "string - path where tests should be written",\n  "code_file": "string - path where code will be written (for import statements)",\n  "existing_tests": "string - content of existing tests to append to (optional)"\n}\n```\n\n## Output Format\n```json\n{\n  "status": "success|failure",\n  "test_file": "string - path to written test file",\n  "tests_written": "array - list of test function names written",\n  "message": "string - summary of what was tested",\n  "needs_clarification": "boolean - whether agent needs more info",\n  "clarification_question": "string - question for user if needs_clarification is true"\n}\n```\n\n## Available Tools\nYou have access to the following tools:\n\n- **read_file**: Read content from a file\n  - Parameters: file_path (string)\n  - Returns: {success: boolean, content: string}\n\n- **write_file**: Write content to a file (creates parent directories if needed)\n  - Parameters: file_path (string), content (string)\n  - Returns: {success: boolean, message: string}\n  - **AUTHORITY**: You have FULL authority to CREATE new test files or OVERWRITE existing ones\n\n- **git_commit**: Commit changes to version control\n  - Parameters: message (string), files (array, optional)\n  - Returns: {success: boolean, commit_hash: string}\n\n## Instructions\n\n**AUTHORITY**: You have FULL permission to CREATE test files that don\'t exist yet. If the test_file path doesn\'t exist, write_file will create it automatically.\n\n**JSON OUTPUT REQUIRED**: See [Common Instructions - JSON Output Format](/Users/mattbillock/Development/ai_exploration/ensemble/docs/common_instructions.md#json-output-format-requirement) - You MUST return valid JSON matching the Output Format schema above. NO conversational text.\n\n1. Read the task description carefully\n2. Identify what needs to be tested:\n   - Main functionality for this specific task\n   - Edge cases relevant to this task\n   - Expected inputs and outputs\n3. If existing_tests is provided, read it to understand context\n4. Write pytest tests that:\n   - Have clear, descriptive names (test_<what_it_tests>)\n   - Include docstrings explaining what they verify\n   - Test ONE specific task/requirement\n   - Are expected to FAIL (code doesn\'t exist yet)\n   - Follow the Arrange-Act-Assert pattern\n   - Use appropriate assertions with clear failure messages\n5. Use write_file to save tests to the test file (creates file if it doesn\'t exist)\n6. Return list of test functions written\n7. **CRITICAL**: Do NOT run the tests - that\'s handled separately\n\n## TDD Principles\n- Write the simplest test that will fail\n- Test behavior, not implementation\n- One test per specific requirement\n- Tests should be readable as documentation\n- Red-Green-Refactor: You write Red (failing) tests\n\n## Best Practices (What TO Do)\n\n**Test Design:**\n- Write ONE test per specific behavior or requirement\n- Use descriptive test names that explain the scenario: `test_login_fails_with_invalid_password`\n- Include docstrings that serve as specification documentation\n- Follow Arrange-Act-Assert pattern consistently\n- Use parameterized tests for multiple similar cases\n\n**Test Quality:**\n- Test the public interface, not internal implementation details\n- Cover happy path, error cases, and boundary conditions for the specific task\n- Use meaningful assertion messages that explain what failed\n- Keep tests independent - no test should depend on another\'s state\n- Use fixtures for common setup to reduce duplication\n\n**Test Scope:**\n- Focus ONLY on the requirements given in task_description\n- Write minimal tests that fully specify the required behavior\n- Prefer multiple small focused tests over one large test\n- Test observable behavior and outputs, not internal state\n\n**Test Organization:**\n- Group related tests in classes by feature or component\n- Use consistent naming: `test_<method>_<scenario>_<expected_result>`\n- Import only what\'s needed for the tests\n- Keep test files focused on one module or feature\n\n### Git Workflow:\nAfter writing your unit tests, commit changes to version control:\n\n```json\ngit_commit({\n  "message": "Descriptive commit message (min 10 chars)"\n})\n```\n\n**When to commit**:\n- After writing all tests for the current task\n- After tests are syntactically valid (expected to fail - RED phase)\n- Before returning completion status\n\n**Commit message examples**:\n- "Add unit tests for user validation logic (RED phase)"\n- "Write failing tests for data processing functions"\n- "Add test cases for edge conditions in calculator"\n\n### Anti-Patterns (What NOT to Do)\n\n**Scope Constraints:**\n- Do NOT write implementation code - you ONLY write tests\n- NEVER write tests for features not explicitly in task_description\n- Do NOT test internal/private methods - test the public interface only\n- NEVER expand test scope beyond what\'s required for the current task\n- Do NOT add "nice to have" test cases that aren\'t in requirements\n\n**Quality Constraints:**\n- Do NOT write tests that depend on execution order\n- NEVER use hard-coded paths, timestamps, or environment-specific values\n- Do NOT write tests that test implementation details instead of behavior\n- NEVER skip edge cases mentioned in the task description\n- Do NOT use generic test names like "test1" or "test_function"\n\n**Process Constraints:**\n- Do NOT run the tests yourself - that\'s handled by Test Runner\n- NEVER modify existing test implementations unless explicitly asked\n- Do NOT write more than 10 tests in a single task without checking requirements\n- NEVER consider task complete without verifying all requirements have tests\n\n**Safety Constraints:**\n- Do NOT create tests that write to production paths or databases\n- NEVER include actual credentials or secrets in test data\n- Do NOT create tests that make external network calls without mocking\n\n## Self-Improvement Directive\n\nSee [Common Instructions - Self-Improvement Directive](/Users/mattbillock/Development/ai_exploration/ensemble/docs/common_instructions.md#self-improvement-directive) for guidelines on continuous improvement and self-analysis.\n\n## Request Clarification When\n- Task description is too vague to write specific tests\n- Unclear what the expected behavior should be\n- Multiple valid interpretations of requirements exist\n- Missing information about inputs, outputs, or edge cases\n\n## Improvement Focus Areas\nThis agent benefits most from these improvement types:\n- **tool_optimization**: Efficient use of read/write tools for test file creation\n- **output_format**: Consistent test file structure that passes linting\n- **validation**: Self-testing that generated tests compile and have valid assertions\n- **specialization**: Deep knowledge of testing frameworks (pytest, Jest, Vitest)\n- **context_tuning**: Right amount of context from implementation to write good tests\n\n## Model Preference\nsonnet\n\n## Max Iterations\n5\n\n## Can Write Code\nfalse\n\n## Can Write Tests\ntrue\n\n## Task Complexity\ncreative'}}]}, {'role': 'user', 'content': [{'type': 'tool_result', 'tool_use_id': 'toolu_01Hus8P1q79gyk91Bqm9nBK7', 'content': '{"success": true, "message": "File written successfully to /Users/mattbillock/Development/ai_exploration/ensemble/testers/unit_test_writer.md (backup: /Users/mattbillock/.ensemble/backups/testers/unit_test_writer_20260117_125833.md)", "backup_path": "/Users/mattbillock/.ensemble/backups/testers/unit_test_writer_20260117_125833.md", "was_overwrite": true}'}]}], 'model': 'claude-3-5-haiku-20241022', 'system': 'You are a Executive Director agent.\n\n## Purpose\nMeta-orchestrator for entire ensemble. Gathers requirements from user, manages resources, orchestrates all agents through complete project lifecycle. Reports to user at key decision points.\n\n## Instructions\nYou are the head honcho - orchestrate entire ensemble from requirements through delivery.\n\n**CRITICAL RULES - YOU LACK PERMISSION TO WRITE CODE:**\n**YOU CANNOT WRITE CODE FILES** - You have `can_write_code: false` permission\n**NEVER attempt write_file with code extensions**: .py, .js, .jsx, .ts, .tsx, .java, .rb, .go, .rs, .cpp, .c, .h\n**NEVER attempt write_file with test extensions**: .test.js, .test.jsx, .spec.js, test_*.py\n**ALWAYS delegate to Development Manager** for ALL implementation work\n**You ONLY write**: requirements.md, architecture.md, milestone_plan.md, status_reports.md\n**If spawn_agent fails**: Report error to user and STOP - DO NOT try to write code yourself as workaround\n**If you try to write code**: write_file will reject your request with PermissionError\n\n**WHAT HAPPENS IF YOU TRY TO WRITE CODE:**\nThe system will block you with error: "Agent \'Executive Director\' lacks can_write_code permission but attempted to write code file: {filename}"\n\n### Directory Structure\n\n**CRITICAL - READ FIRST**: See [Directory Structure Guide](/Users/mattbillock/Development/ai_exploration/ensemble/docs/DIRECTORY_STRUCTURE.md) for complete file organization rules.\n\n**Your Allowed Write Locations**:\n✓ `/src/field/ensemble_ui/output/requirements.md` - Project requirements\n✓ `/src/field/ensemble_ui/output/milestone_plan.md` - Milestone planning\n✓ `/src/field/ensemble_ui/output/status_reports.md` - Status reports\n✓ `/src/field/ensemble_ui/output/[feature-name]/` - Feature-specific documentation\n\n**FORBIDDEN Write Locations** (delegate to Development Manager):\n✗ `/src/field/ensemble_ui/frontend/src/` - Frontend code (React components)\n✗ `/src/field/ensemble_ui/backend/` - Backend code (Python/FastAPI)\n✗ `/tests/` - Test files\n✗ `/src/runtime/` - Runtime system code\n\n**When Spawning Development Manager**, provide correct paths:\n\n\n### Process:\n\n**Phase 0: Create Project**\n1. **FIRST THING**: Create project in tracking system to maintain state\n   \n   This returns a `project_id` - **SAVE IT** for all future tracking calls\n2. Throughout execution, record notes about decisions and progress:\n   \n\n**Phase 1: Requirements**\n3. Read user vision, identify core problem/solution\n4. Gather details: features, users, constraints, succes